# Sales Commission Plan — FY26: clawback

> Module Clawback

This module computes how much paid commission is recoverable under S-7. It
computes the *quantum* only. "The Company may recover" in S-7.1 is an election
about enforcement, and the choice between deduction from future payments and
suing as a debt is a remedy, not an amount; neither is modelled, because a
module that decided whether the Company will in fact recover would be asserting
a commercial decision it cannot see.

Whether the contract has become a Churned Contract is determined by S-2.4,
which is encoded once, in `Commission.ChurnedContract`. It arrives here as the
boolean `is_churned_contract` rather than being re-derived, so there is exactly
one definition of churn in the system.

**Judgement input.** S-7.2 turns on a "material breach by the Company", which
is a legal characterisation the Plan does not define and no record decides. It
is lifted to the input `terminated_for_company_material_breach` and the module
computes the consequence *given* that determination. The module must not and
does not infer it from anything else — not from the churn, not from the timing,
not from the size of the contract.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration scope RecoverableCommission:
  input commission_paid content money
  input payment_date content date
  input assessment_date content date
  input is_churned_contract content boolean
  input terminated_for_company_material_breach content boolean
  output long_stop_date content date
  output recovery_time_barred content boolean
  output recoverable_amount content money
```

## S-7 Clawback

| COMM-PLAN S-7.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:92)
|
| Where commission has been paid in respect of a contract which
| subsequently becomes a Churned Contract, the Company may recover the commission
| attributable to that contract by deduction from future commission payments or,
| where no such payments fall due, as a debt.

The base case is that nothing is recoverable: the Plan creates no general right
to recover paid commission, and S-7.1 is the only clause that creates one. So
`no_recovery` is the root of the chain and S-7.1 is an exception to it,
conditioned on the contract having become a Churned Contract. The amount is the
commission attributable to that contract, which the caller supplies as
`commission_paid`.

The amount recoverable is bounded below by the clause that creates the right.
S-7.1 operates "where commission **has been paid**", and what it permits the
Company to recover is "the commission attributable to that contract" — a sum
that has actually been paid out. A sum paid is nought or more, so a negative
`commission_paid` describes no payment the Plan could be recovering; recovered,
it would be a payment *to* the employee dressed as a clawback, made by the one
clause in the Plan that confers no entitlement to anything. The bound is
therefore S-7.1's own and not a programming convenience, and the scope declines
rather than computing a negative recovery.

```catala
scope RecoverableCommission:
  assertion commission_paid >= $0

  label no_recovery definition recoverable_amount equals $0

```

| COMM-PLAN S-7.2 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:97)
|
| By way of exception to S-7.1, no recovery will be made where the
| contract was terminated by the customer by reason of a material breach by the
| Company.

An exception to S-7.1, encoded as one. The condition repeats S-7.1's
`is_churned_contract`: a Catala exception does not inherit its parent's
condition, and without the repetition this definition would fire for a contract
that never churned at all. The value would still be `$0` there, but the answer
would then be attributed to S-7.2 — reporting that recovery was barred by a
Company breach where in truth no recovery right ever arose.

The clause's factual limb ("terminated by the customer") is carried inside the
judgement input as the ledger names it, rather than split into a separate
boolean, so that the whole characterisation S-7.2 requires is decided by a
human in one place.

```catala
scope RecoverableCommission:
  label s7_2 exception s7_1 definition recoverable_amount
    under condition
      is_churned_contract and terminated_for_company_material_breach
    consequence equals $0
```

| COMM-PLAN S-7.3 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:101)
|
| No recovery will be made more than 24 months after the date on which
| the commission was paid.

The long-stop runs from the date the commission was **paid**, not from the
signature date and not from the churn event. "More than 24 months after" is
strict, so an assessment made exactly 24 months after payment is still in time
and one made a day later is not.

It sits below `s7_2` in the chain for a structural reason: both bars produce
`$0`, and two sibling exceptions to `s7_1` whose conditions can both hold would
be a runtime `Conflict` for a contract that both churned through a Company
breach and aged past the long-stop. Placing S-7.3 last also states the right
precedence — the long-stop is absolute. It repeats S-7.1's
`is_churned_contract` for the same provenance reason as S-7.2, but deliberately
does **not** repeat S-7.2's judgement condition: the long-stop applies whether
or not there was any material breach.

`date round down` is declared because 24 months after 29 February is a date
that does not exist and Catala refuses to guess; rounding down puts the
long-stop on 28 February, which is the reading that does not extend the
Company's recovery window.

```catala
scope RecoverableCommission:
  date round down

  definition long_stop_date equals payment_date + 24 month

  definition recovery_time_barred equals assessment_date > long_stop_date

  label s7_3 exception s7_2 definition recoverable_amount
    under condition is_churned_contract and recovery_time_barred
    consequence equals $0
```
